: "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "The group's partner set is replaced wholesale around t=23370: edges before and after the 5000-unit half-windows share zero overlap (126 removed, 106 added), whereas the same group retains ~46-48% of its partners across equivalent within-regime window pairs earlier and later. The old cohort (65,74,163,292,442,880,978,1587,1888,1999) was highly active up to the change point then goes silent; the new cohort (66,232,245,422,526,702,848,986,990,1566) is absent beforehand. Group event volume and per-node degree are stable across the boundary, and the group members are persistently top-active, so neither volume nor popularity explains the shift. A brief activity gap (23000-25500) separates the two regimes, consistent with a short transition rather than gradual drift."}, "raw_final_response": "All evidence lines up. Key findings:\n\n**Change point \u2248 t=23370** (peak turnover 1.0, step_02), with an abrupt sequence: turnover rises 0.17\u21921.0 between t\u224820300 and 23370 and returns to ~0.3 by 28000 \u2014 a sharp spike, not a plateau of gradual drift.\n\n**Complete contact substitution:** across [18370,23370] vs [23371,28370] the group's edges have Jaccard = 0.0, 126 removed / 106 added (step_04), while group activity volume and degree are essentially unchanged (13\u201319 \u2192 8\u201317 events; step_05, step_06). So it's a rewiring, not a volume change.\n\n**Baseline turnover is much lower:** within-regime comparisons give Jaccard 0.48 (step_09, pre-change) and 0.46 (step_15, post-change), so the group normally retains ~half its partners. Zero overlap at 23370 is a clear outlier.\n\n**Partner-side confirmation:** old partners {65,74,163,292,442,880,978,1587,1888,1999} were highly active before (21\u201328 events each, step_12) and nearly silent after (0\u20132 events, step_07); new partners {66,232,245,422,526,...} were essentially absent before (0\u20131 events, step_14). The two cohorts are temporally disjoint.\n\n**Confounder checks:** the graph is globally sparse/non-repeating (repeat_ratio 0.156, step_01), so the graph-wide Jaccard of 0.0 across the same windows (step_10) is the null baseline \u2014 what distinguishes the group is that it *does* normally repeat (~0.46\u20130.48) and only drops to 0 here. Group event counts stay flat (~90\u2013107/bin) apart from a quiet gap at 23000\u201325500 (step_03, step_11), so mere popularity/volume doesn't explain it; the group members are top-active throughout (step_13).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_798d04c6\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163, 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763],\n  \"window\": [18370, 28370],\n  \"confidence\": 0.89,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The group's partner set is replaced wholesale around t=23370: edges before and after the 5000-unit half-windows share zero overlap (126 removed, 106 added), whereas the same group retains ~46-48% of its partners across equivalent within-regime window pairs earlier and later. The old cohort (65,74,163,292,442,880,978,1587,1888,1999) was highly active up to the change point then goes silent; the new cohort (66,232,245,422,526,702,848,986,990,1566) is absent beforehand. Group event volume and per-node degree are stable across the boundary, and the group members are persistently top-active, so neither volume nor popularity explains the shift. A brief activity gap (23000-25500) separates the two regimes, consistent with a short transition rather than gradual drift.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 65683, "total_output_tokens": 6571, "total_tokens": 72254, "total_latency_ms": 95946, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}